L000482	The saddle point is characterized by exactly one negative eigenvalue ($\lambda'_1 < 0$), confirming it is a first-order saddle point with the unstable mode along the reaction coordinate. Both configurations share a zero-frequency mode arising from the continuous rotational symmetry about the $z$-axis. As discussed in the main text, this mode is excluded from the determinant ratio in the K-L rate calculation.
L000483	
L000484	
L000485	
L000486	\section{Molecular dynamics simulations}
L000487	
L000488	To obtain the theoretical inversion rates, we perform extensive MD simulations by integrating the equations of motion for the 5-ion cluster. The ions are treated as point charges with mass $m$ interacting via the Coulomb force and the external trap potential. The interaction with the laser cooling field is modeled using the Langevin equation:
L000489	\begin{equation}
L000490	m \ddot{\mathbf{r}}_i = -\nabla_i V(\{\mathbf{r}\}) - \gamma \dot{\mathbf{r}}_i + \boldsymbol{\xi}_i(t),
L000491	\end{equation}
L000492	where $\gamma$ is the friction coefficient and $\boldsymbol{\xi}_i(t)$ is a stochastic force satisfying the fluctuation-dissipation theorem $\langle \xi_{i\alpha}(t) \xi_{j\beta}(t') \rangle = 2 m \gamma k_B T \delta_{ij} \delta_{\alpha\beta} \delta(t-t')$.
L000493	
L000494	
L000495	
L000496	\subsection{Numerical integration}
L000497	We use a modified Velocity Verlet integrator adapted for Langevin dynamics \cite{Jensen_2013}. The simulation proceeds in two phases. First, a damping phase ($t < t_{\text{damp}}$) where the system is initialized with random positions and a strong damping force is applied without thermal noise to rapidly settle the ions into the local minimum. Second, a thermal phase where the friction is set to the experimental cooling value ($\gamma / m \approx 6 \times 10^3 \, \text{s}^{-1}$) and the stochastic thermal force is activated. The trajectories are evolved for $10^9$ steps with a time step $\Delta t = 10^{-8}$ s.
L000498	
L000499	
L000500	
L000501	
L000502	\begin{figure}[h]
L000503	\centering
L000504	\begin{minipage}{0.95\linewidth} 
L000505	    \hrule height 1pt \vspace{3pt}
L000506	    \textbf{Algorithm 1:} Langevin Dynamics for Ion Inversion
L000507	    \vspace{3pt} \hrule \vspace{5pt}
L000508	    
L000509	    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
L000510	        \State \textbf{Initialize:} Random positions $\mathbf{r}$ and velocities $\mathbf{v}$.
L000511	        \State \textbf{Parameters:} $\Delta t = 10^{-8}$\,s, $N_{\text{steps}} = 10^9$, \texttt{save\_interval = 100}, Temperature $T$.
L000512	        \State \textbf{Define Forces:} $\mathbf{F}_{\text{trap}}(\mathbf{r}) = - \nabla V_{\text{trap}}$, $\mathbf{F}_{\text{coul}}(\mathbf{r}) = - \nabla V_{\text{coul}}$.
L000513	        \State
L000514	        \For{$step = 1$ to $N_{\text{steps}}$}
L000515	            \If{$step < N_{\text{damp}}$} \Comment{Initial Damping}
L000516	                \State $\mathbf{F}_{\text{total}} = \mathbf{F}_{\text{trap}} + \mathbf{F}_{\text{coul}} - \gamma_{\text{high}} \mathbf{v}$
L000517	                \State Integrate equations of motion (Damped Verlet).
L000518	            \Else \Comment{Langevin Dynamics}
L000519	                \State Generate Gaussian noise $\boldsymbol{\xi}$ with variance $\sigma^2 = 2 m \gamma k_B T \Delta t$.
L000520	                \State $\mathbf{F}_{\text{total}} = \mathbf{F}_{\text{trap}} + \mathbf{F}_{\text{coul}} - \gamma \mathbf{v} + \boldsymbol{\xi}$
L000521	                \State Update $\mathbf{r}(t+\Delta t)$ and $\mathbf{v}(t+\Delta t)$.
L000522	            \EndIf
L000523	            \State
L000524	            \If{$step \pmod{\text{save\_interval}} == 0$}
L000525	                \State Store $\psi_{30}$ of current configuration.
L000526	            \EndIf
L000527	        \EndFor
L000528	        \State \textbf{Post-Process:} Obtain dwell times $\tau$ from $\psi_{30}$ sign flips. Extract rate from the histogram of dwell times (Fig.~\ref{fig:dwell_times}).
L000529	    \end{algorithmic}
L000530	    
L000531	    \vspace{5pt} \hrule
L000532	\end{minipage}
L000533	\label{alg:langevin}
L000534	\end{figure}
L000535	
L000536	\subsection{Extracting dwell times: barrier recrossings}
L000537	We track the octupole moment $\psi_{30}$ of the cluster to identify inversion events, which are flagged by a change in its sign. Due to the low friction (underdamped regime), the system often recrosses the barrier multiple times before settling into the new potential well. These short-time recrossings do not constitute a successful reaction event. 
L000538	
L000539	
L000540	
L000541	\begin{figure}[tbp]
L000542	    \centering
L000543	    \includegraphics[width=0.65\linewidth]{histogram.pdf}
L000544	    \caption{\textbf{Dwell time distribution from MD simulations.} Histogram of time intervals between inversion events from MD simulations. The large count in the first bin corresponds to rapid barrier recrossings (see Fig.~\ref{fig2} in the main text). The rate $\kappa$ is extracted by fitting an exponential decay from the second bin of the histogram ($\tau \gg m/\gamma$).}
L000545	    \label{fig:dwell_times}
